# English changes what it is handed, and British English most of all: an r
# after a vowel is left out and one is put in between a word that ends in a
# vowel and a word that begins with one, a t before an r or a y becomes an
# affricate, a vowel before an r gains a schwa. American English says a t or
# a d between vowels as a flap. That is English, and a module that is to say
# another language's phones, or another dialect's, has to say them as they
# were given: eSpeak NG has done the dialect's own phonology already.
rule pre_syll_phone_adjustments takes 3 from et_phnol.obj
  afresh
  call ZZfence_null
  match
end

rule post_syll_adjustments takes 3 from et_phnol.obj
  afresh
  call ZZfence_null
  match
end

rule eng_phrase_level_rules takes 3 from et_phnol.obj
  afresh
  call ZZfence_null
  match
end
